# Intent: a unit that cannot hear the wake word finds out and repairs itself (owner report 2026-11-13, "stackchan is not listening to me ... It should be self healing"; design note docs/dev/stackchan-self-healing-2026-11-13.md, rung (c)). "Speech heard for N minutes and no detection" proves nothing (nobody may have said the word), and playing the wake word through the unit's own speaker is what the echo canceller is built to remove. What does prove the engine is an injection test. (1) The host's "system wake_selftest" command, accepted only while the unit is idle and the wake word is armed, feeds a clip of the wake word (wake_selftest.pcm, 16 kHz mono 16-bit, packed into the assets image by recipe.sh from firmware/assets/) into the wake front end digitally, as channel M with silence on R, in place of the microphone for the clip and one more second. A WAKENET_DETECTED during it is counted as a hit and NOT passed to the application (no state change, no tone, no turn). The result is a "wake_selftest" event: result=hit or miss (hits, ms) or skipped (busy, stopped). It proves the model, the front end and WakeNet, not the microphone. (2) "system wake_rebuild" makes the front end again in place (AFE destroy and create from the same configuration, on the detection task between two fetches, about 400 ms): it resets the echo canceller and WakeNet's state, which is the cure under the echo-canceller theory of the deafness; a "wake_selftest" event with result=rebuilt confirms it. The host decides the ladder: test 60 s after each turn and hourly while idle, a miss rebuilds and retests, a second miss restarts through the guard of patch 085. (3) The microphone gets its own number: wake_stats gains mic_rms_max (the loudest echo-cancelled chunk's RMS in the minute; a microphone that hears people reads far above a dead one), selftest_ok, selftest_miss and rebuilds (totals since boot).
